### Using SpEL Expressions in Repository Query Methods

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-data-jpa/blob/main/src/main/antora/modules/ROOT/pages/jpa/query-methods.adoc

Shows how to use the #{#entityName} SpEL expression in a @Query annotation to dynamically reference the entity name, making queries more adaptable to entity name changes or configurations.

```java
@Entity
public class User {

  @Id
  @GeneratedValue
  Long id;

  String lastname;
}

public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User,Long> {

  @Query("select u from #{#entityName} u where u.lastname = ?1")
  List<User> findByLastname(String lastname);
}
```

--------------------------------

### Using SpEL entityName Expression in Repository Query Methods

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-data-jpa/blob/main/src/main/antora/modules/ROOT/pages/jpa/query-methods.adoc

Defines a generic repository interface with a query that dynamically uses the entity name via #{#entityName}. Useful for inheritance scenarios to avoid repeating query definitions.

```java
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class AbstractMappedType {
  …
  String attribute;
}

@Entity
public class ConcreteType extends AbstractMappedType { … }

@NoRepositoryBean
public interface MappedTypeRepository<T extends AbstractMappedType>
  extends Repository<T, Long> {

  @Query("select t from #{#entityName} t where t.attribute = ?1")
  List<T> findAllByAttribute(String attribute);
}

public interface ConcreteRepository
  extends MappedTypeRepository<ConcreteType> { … }
```

--------------------------------

### Query Method Using Record DTO Projection

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-data-jpa/blob/main/spring-data-jpa/spring-data-jpa/src/test/java/org/springframework/data/jpa/repository/sample/UserRepository.java

Repository method using the @Query annotation with GROUP BY to return grouped aggregate results as a record DTO projection.

```java
@UserRoleCountProjectingQuery
List<UserRoleCountDtoProjection> dtoProjectionEntityAndAggregatedValue();

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Query("select u, count(r) from User u left outer join u.roles r group by u")
@interface UserRoleCountProjectingQuery {
}
```

### JPA > Query Methods > Using SpEL expressions in Repository Query Methods: entityName with Inheritance

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-data-jpa/blob/main/src/main/antora/modules/ROOT/pages/jpa/query-methods.adoc

The `#{#entityName}` SpEL expression in a query string refers to the entity name, which is useful for handling potential remappings of the entity class (e.g., via `@Entity(name = "MyUser")`). This expression is also valuable when defining generic repository interfaces with specialized interfaces for concrete domain types, allowing custom query methods to be defined once in the generic interface without repetition.

--------------------------------

### Projections > Using Projections with JPA > String-based queries > JPQL Queries

Source: https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-data-jpa/blob/main/src/main/antora/modules/ROOT/pages/repositories/projections.adoc

For string-based queries (JPQL and native), class-based projections require specific considerations. JPQL uses constructor expressions for class-based projections, requiring a query like `SELECT new com.example.NamesOnly(u.firstname, u.lastname) from User u`. Spring Data JPA can rewrite queries to use constructor expressions if they select the root object, individual properties, or DTO objects. This rewriting is bypassed if the `@Query`-annotated query already uses constructor expressions. Ensure DTO types have an all-args constructor for projection.

